    "q-112299": {
      "dom_id": "step-q112299",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-{t}^{2}-12t-7$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({t}^{2}+12t+7)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}+12t+7\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}+2\\cdot 6t+7\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $7$ last:\n\n$$\n{{t}^{2}+2\\cdot 6t+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-{6}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(t+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(t+6)^{2}-29\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7-12t-{t}^{2} & =-((t+6)^{2}-29) \\\\  & =-(t+6)^{2}+29 \\\\  & =29-(t+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
