    "q-218604": {
      "dom_id": "step-q218604",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+12x+14)$ by completing the square.\n\n$-({x}^{2}+12x+14)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-(x+$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}$",
      "worked_solution": "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+12x+14\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+14\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{6}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-22.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+12x+14) & =-({(x+6)}^{2}-22) \\\\  & =-(x+6)^{2}+22 \\\\  & =22-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
          "tag": "mathquill",
          "choices": [],
