    "q-140270": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140270",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-3x+1)$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-3x+1) & =-({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-140270-a",
