    "q-140332": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140332",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-x-2)$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x-2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-x-2) & =-({(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
