    "q-142072": {
      "dom_id": "step-q142072",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{1}{4}$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $-\\frac{1}{4}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{1}{4} & =-({(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{41}{2}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-142072-a",
