    "q-140372": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140372",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Rewrite $4{x}^{2}+24x$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n4({x}^{2}+6x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+6x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-{3}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+3)}^{2}-{3}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+3)}^{2}-9.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+6x) & =4({(x+3)}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =4{(x+3)}^{2}-4\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =4{(x+3)}^{2}-36\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
