    "q-140528": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140528",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Rewrite $2{x}^{2}-12x+16$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2({x}^{2}-6x+8)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-{3}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-6x+8) & =2((x-3)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =2{(x-3)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2{(x-3)}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
