      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Rewrite $8{x}^{2}-4x$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, we take out a factor of $8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n8({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x) & =8({(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
