    "q-218760": {
      "dom_id": "step-q218760",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-3{y}^{2}+8y-6$ by completing the square.\n\n$-3{y}^{2}+8y-6=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3.$ So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2) & =-3({(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
          "tag": "mathquill",
