    "q-140536": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140536",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-5{x}^{2}-6x+\\frac{1}{5}$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5.$ So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot x-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{1}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}) & =-5({(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
