    "q-140380": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140380",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-5{y}^{2}+2y+\\frac{4}{5}$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5.$ So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{4}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}) & =-5({(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
