    "q-140376": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140376",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-7{x}^{2}-14x+7$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-7.$ So, we take out a factor of $-7$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-7({x}^{2}+2x-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x-1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+1)}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7({x}^{2}+2x-1) & =-7({(x+1)}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-7{(x+1)}^{2}+(-7)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-7{(x+1)}^{2}+14\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-140376-a",
